The quintessential American dessert created at the Parker House Hotel in Boston. Despite its name, it is a tender double-layer sponge cake sandwiching rich egg-rich vanilla custard, draped with dark chocolate ganache.

*Adapted from [King Arthur Baking](https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/boston-cream-pie-recipe).*

## Ingredients

### Hot Milk Sponge Cake
- 2 cups (397g) granulated sugar
- 4 large eggs
- ⅓ cup (67g) vegetable oil
- 2 cups (240g) King Arthur Unbleached All-Purpose Flour
- 1 ¼ teaspoons table salt
- 2 teaspoons baking powder
- 4 tablespoons (57g) unsalted butter, at cool room temperature
- 1 cup (227g) whole milk
- 2 teaspoons pure vanilla extract

### Vanilla Pastry Cream Filling
- 2 ½ cups (567g) whole milk, divided
- ½ cup (99g) granulated sugar
- ¼ teaspoon table salt
- ⅓ cup (39g) cornstarch
- 3 large egg yolks
- 1 large whole egg
- 2 teaspoons pure vanilla extract

### Chocolate Ganache Glaze
- ¼ cup (57g) heavy whipping cream
- ⅓ cup (57g) dark chocolate chips or chopped semisweet chocolate
- ½ teaspoon pure vanilla extract

## Instructions

1. **Bake the Hot-Milk Sponge Cake:**
   - Preheat oven to 325°F (165°C). Lightly grease two 9-inch round cake pans and fit the bottoms with rounds of parchment paper.
   - Using an electric mixer on medium-high speed, whip the granulated sugar and eggs for about 2 minutes until voluminous, pale, and thick. Drizzle in the vegetable oil and beat to combine.
   - In a medium bowl, whisk together the flour, salt, and baking powder. Add this dry blend to the egg mixture, beating on low speed just until smooth.
   - Heat the 1 cup of milk and 4 tablespoons butter in a small saucepan over medium heat until small bubbles form around the perimeter. Pull from the heat and stir in 2 teaspoons vanilla extract.
   - Slowly pour the hot milk mixture into the batter in a steady stream, beating on low speed until smooth and fluid (the batter will have a runny consistency).
   - Divide the batter evenly between both prepared pans. Bake for 30 to 35 minutes until the centers bounce back when touched and a wooden toothpick comes out clean. Rest pans for 10 minutes, then invert cakes onto cooling racks and discard parchment.

2. **Prepare the Silky Vanilla Pastry Cream:**
   - In a saucepan, warm 2 cups of the milk, ½ cup sugar, and ¼ teaspoon salt over medium heat until steaming, stirring until sugar dissolves.
   - In a heatproof mixing bowl, whisk together the cornstarch, egg yolks, whole egg, and remaining ½ cup cold milk until lump-free.
   - Gradually ladle about half of the hot milk into the egg mixture while whisking vigorously to gently warm the eggs without curdling them.
   - Pour the tempered mixture back into the saucepan through a fine-mesh sieve to catch any bits of cooked egg.
   - Cook over medium heat, whisking constantly across the bottom and corners of the pan, until large bubbles burst and the custard thickens into a velvety pudding (about 2 minutes). Remove from the heat and stir in 2 teaspoons vanilla extract.
   - Scrape into a clean bowl, lay plastic wrap directly against the surface of the cream to prevent a skin, and refrigerate until thoroughly chilled.

3. **Assemble the Cake:**
   - Place one cooled sponge layer flat-side down onto your serving platter.
   - Whisk the chilled pastry cream briefly to smooth its texture, then spoon it onto the cake, spreading outward while leaving a ½-inch border around the perimeter.
   - Place the second sponge cake layer gently on top, pressing lightly to secure the sandwich.

4. **Glaze with Dark Chocolate:**
   - Combine the heavy cream and chopped chocolate in a small microwave-safe bowl. Warm in 20-second bursts, stirring between each, until silky and melted. Mix in the ½ teaspoon vanilla.
   - Let the chocolate ganache rest at room temperature for 10 minutes to thicken slightly into a spreadable glaze.
   - Pour the ganache directly onto the center of the top cake layer. Gently encourage it outward with a spatula so it drapes naturally down the sides.
   - Chill the pie for at least 30 minutes in the refrigerator so the ganache firms up before slicing.

## Notes

- **Make-Ahead Friendly:** The pastry cream can be made up to 2 days in advance and kept refrigerated.
- **Storage:** Store leftovers covered in the refrigerator for up to 4 days.
